WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 9.1.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.320
Same idea, different machine.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.285
By the end of this lesson, you'll

00:00:06.285 --> 00:00:08.480
be able to explain why a concentrated

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.676
liquidity position and a grid bot are

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:13.565
economically close cousins, and where they differ.

00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:16.155
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:17.840
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.555
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:20.555 --> 00:00:22.080
Then, your checklist.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.242
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:25.542 --> 00:00:28.870
Both strategies do the same basic thing inside a

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:32.063
price range: sell as price rises, buy as price

00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:35.827
falls, and profit from price moving back and forth.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:39.542
How it trades, Discrete orders at set levels, Continuous.

00:00:39.542 --> 00:00:42.382
Every trade in range moves your inventory.

00:00:42.382 --> 00:00:44.986
What it earns, Spread between grid levels,

00:00:44.986 --> 00:00:47.469
minus exchange fees, Share of the pool's

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:49.767
swap fee on every trade in range.

00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.651
Who pays whom, You pay trading

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.592
fees, Traders pay you fees.

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:56.975
At the range low, Holds mostly/all the base

00:00:56.975 --> 00:01:00.042
asset, Holds 100% of the base asset.

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:04.230
At the range high, Holds mostly/all quote

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.817
(USDT/USDC), Holds 100% of the quote asset.

00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:11.785
Outside the range, Stops trading, holding one

00:01:11.785 --> 00:01:14.237
side, Stops earning, holding one side.

00:01:15.537 --> 00:01:19.409
Custody, Exchange holds funds (you keep API control), You

00:01:19.409 --> 00:01:22.847
hold keys, and the contract holds the position.

00:01:22.847 --> 00:01:26.260
Main extra risk, Exchange / counterparty,

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.242
Smart contract, oracle, MEV, gas.

00:01:29.242 --> 00:01:32.165
Automation, Built into the tool, Manual, or

00:01:32.165 --> 00:01:35.087
a third-party manager (extra contract risk).

00:01:36.387 --> 00:01:38.902
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:38.902 --> 00:01:46.052
Range $2,700 to $3,300, ETH at $3,000.

00:01:46.052 --> 00:01:52.601
Price falls to $2,700: geometric average the square

00:01:52.601 --> 00:01:58.064
root of (2,700 times 3,000) about $2,846.

00:01:58.064 --> 00:02:03.609
Price rises to $3,300: Almost the same inventory path.

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:06.396
The real difference is how money is made

00:02:06.396 --> 00:02:09.387
on the way: grid profit per completed level

00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:12.581
(after paying fees), versus L.P. fee income on

00:02:12.581 --> 00:02:16.484
all trading volume in range (after impermanent loss).

00:02:16.484 --> 00:02:21.726
Grid (20 arithmetic levels, $30 apart):

00:02:21.726 --> 00:02:24.609
buys at 2,970, 2,940 …

00:02:25.309 --> 00:02:31.039
2,700 to average buy about $2,835.

00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:35.663
Concentrated L.P.: converts U.S.D.C. to ETH continuously

00:02:35.663 --> 00:02:37.829
on the way down at the.

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:39.864
Grid: sells at 3,030 …

00:02:40.564 --> 00:02:45.919
3,300 to average about $3,165.

00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:50.550
L.P.: sells ETH at the square root

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:55.314
of (3,000 times 3,300) about $3,146.

00:02:56.614 --> 00:02:58.700
Let's look at the diagram:

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.164
Grid bot vs concentrated LP.

00:03:01.164 --> 00:03:02.724
Take a moment with it.

00:03:02.724 --> 00:03:05.134
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:03:06.434 --> 00:03:07.894
Here's your checklist.

00:03:07.894 --> 00:03:09.429
Do it now, for real.

00:03:09.429 --> 00:03:12.334
First, I can explain why both end up

00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:15.304
100% in one asset at a range edge.

00:03:15.304 --> 00:03:17.018
Next, I know which one pays

00:03:17.018 --> 00:03:19.154
fees and which one earns them.

00:03:19.154 --> 00:03:21.385
And finally, I treat a range break

00:03:21.385 --> 00:03:23.489
as an invalidation event in both.

00:03:24.789 --> 00:03:25.999
Question 1.

00:03:25.999 --> 00:03:27.894
Price breaks below the range.

00:03:27.894 --> 00:03:29.306
What do the grid bot

00:03:29.306 --> 00:03:30.719
and the LP both hold?

00:03:35.139 --> 00:03:37.225
The answer: All, or nearly all, the

00:03:37.225 --> 00:03:39.554
base asset, bought on the way down.

00:03:39.554 --> 00:03:42.709
Both stop earning until price returns or you act.

00:03:44.009 --> 00:03:45.254
Question 2.

00:03:45.254 --> 00:03:46.899
Who pays trading fees in each?

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:54.441
The answer: The grid bot pays exchange fees.

00:03:54.441 --> 00:03:56.756
The LP earns fees from traders.

00:03:58.057 --> 00:03:59.327
Question 3.

00:03:59.327 --> 00:04:01.331
Name one risk the LP has that

00:04:01.331 --> 00:04:03.802
the grid bot doesn't, and vice versa.

00:04:08.102 --> 00:04:13.242
The answer: LP: smart-contract/MEV/gas risk.

00:04:13.242 --> 00:04:16.087
Grid bot: exchange/counterparty risk.

00:04:17.387 --> 00:04:19.902
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:19.902 --> 00:04:22.887
Next up, Lesson 9.2: When each one wins.
